course: everyday
lesson: 54
title: Solving disagreements calmly
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Disagreements happen between all people, even best friends and loving families; they are a normal part of life, and what matters is how we solve them.
explain: First cool down: an angry brain shouts and grabs, while a calm brain can think and listen, so stop, breathe and take a short break if you need one.
explain: Then use words, not hands: say how you feel with an «I» sentence («I feel upset when you take my pens without asking»), and then listen to the other side without interrupting.
explain: Last, look for a fair way that works for both: take turns, share, split the time, choose together, or meet in the middle, which is called a compromise.
explain: Two brothers in Melbourne both want the TV; after cooling down, they agree that one watches his show now and the other picks the film after dinner, and nobody has to cry.
explain: Two boys in Leeds started arguing over a ball and it turned into a shove; one fell, hit his head on the ground and needed stitches.
explain: A girl in California sent an angry message in the middle of a row; it was shared around the class, and the friendship took months to mend.
fact: Disagreements are normal, even between good friends.
fact: It is easier to solve a problem when everyone is calm.
fact: Hitting, kicking or grabbing never solves a disagreement fairly.
fact: An «I» sentence says how you feel without blaming the other person.
fact: Listening to the other side helps you understand why they are upset.
fact: A compromise is when each side gives a little so that both are fairly happy.
fact: Taking turns, sharing and splitting time are fair ways to solve many quarrels; when nobody saw clearly what happened in a game, doing it again, such as replaying the point, is fair.
fact: A real apology says what you did wrong and does not add an excuse.
fact: If you cannot solve a problem, or someone is getting hurt, you ask an adult to help.
fact: Sometimes both people are a little bit right.
fact: Flipping a coin is a fair way to decide when both choices are equally good.
fact: A small argument that turns into pushing can end with someone badly hurt.
fact: Things said or sent in anger are hard to take back.
exercise: Why should you cool down before trying to solve a quarrel? | A calm mind can think and listen much better | So the other person forgets the problem and you don't have to talk | Because cooling down means you win | So that you have time to plan revenge
exercise: Which is an «I» sentence? | I feel sad when you call me names | You are always so mean to me | Everyone knows you are a liar | You never let anybody play
exercise: Why does an «I» sentence work better than blaming? | It explains your feeling without making them want to argue back | It proves that you are right | It makes the other person feel guilty | It ends the talk quickly so you can leave and go and do something else
exercise: What is a compromise? | Each side gives a little so both are fairly happy | One side gets everything it wants | Both sides give up, go home and stay cross with each other | An adult decides who is right
exercise: Two friends want to play different games at break. What is a fair solution? | Play one game first and the other game after | Play the game of whoever is louder | Each plays alone and stays cross for the rest of the day | Play the game of whoever is older
exercise: Why does hitting never solve a disagreement fairly? | It hurts someone and the stronger person just wins | It makes the problem disappear forever | It is fair if they started it first and hit you before | It works only if no adult sees it
exercise: Why is it important to listen to the other side? | You may learn why they are upset and find a fix | So you can find all the mistakes in what they say | It is just being polite, but it never helps at all | So they will have to listen to you next time instead
exercise: When should you ask an adult to help with a disagreement? | When you cannot solve it or someone is getting hurt | Every single time anyone at all disagrees with you about anything | Never, because it is like tattling | Only when you are sure you are right
exercise: Which is a real apology? | I am sorry I broke your pencil, I will get you a new one | I am sorry, but you started it | Sorry you feel that way | Sorry, but it was only a pencil and you have loads more of them
exercise: How can two children fairly share one bicycle for an afternoon? | Take turns with a timer, each the same time | The owner of the bike rides it all day | The faster cyclist rides it more | They both stay off it all afternoon so that nobody gets upset
exercise: You think you are right, but your friend has a good point too. What is wise? | Admit that both of you are partly right | Keep arguing until your friend finally gives in | Pretend you did not hear the good point at all | Change the subject so you win the argument
exercise: When is flipping a coin a fair way to decide? | When both choices are equally good and you cannot agree | When one person clearly did something wrong to the other | When someone has been hurt | When you want to win by luck every time
exercise: What does it mean to walk away to cool down? | Taking a short break, then coming back to talk | Leaving and never talking about it again | Running off to tell everyone about the fight | Hiding so the other person feels bad
exercise: Why can disagreeing with a friend sometimes be useful? | It can help you both understand each other better | It proves which one of you is the cleverer friend | It is never useful in any way | It means the friendship is ending
test: Mia and Josh in Austin both grab the last red crayon. What would be the best first thing to do? | Let go, calm down and talk about it | Pull harder and harder until one of them wins | Snap the crayon in two so nobody gets it | Run to the teacher before saying a word
test: Which sentence is most likely to calm a quarrel down? | I feel left out when you two whisper | You two are so rude and mean | I am never playing with either of you again | Nobody likes you anyway
test: Two sisters want to choose the family film. Last week the older one chose. What is fair? | The younger one chooses this week | The older one chooses again this week | They choose nothing and watch nothing | The one who argues longest chooses
test: What would probably happen if two friends shouted at each other without listening? | Neither would understand the other and the quarrel would grow | They would make up much faster | The louder friend would turn out right | They would both soon forget what the quarrel was about and make friends again
test: Which is true about compromise? | Both sides are usually fairly happy, though not completely | One side always loses everything | It only works between adults | It means the person who is wrong wins and the person who is right loses
test: A boy says «Sorry, but you made me do it». Why is this not a good apology? | It blames the other person instead of owning up | It is too short to count | It uses the word sorry too early | It should be said in writing instead, so it can be kept
test: In a game, two players each say the ball was in or out and nobody saw clearly. What is a fair way to decide? | Replay the point | The stronger player decides | The player who shouts loudest decides | Stop the whole game for good
test: Why should you ask an adult when a quarrel turns into pushing? | Someone could get hurt and needs help stopping it | Because adults enjoy sorting out fights | Because the pushing child will be punished | Because children can never solve any quarrels on their own
test: After a disagreement is solved, what helps friends move on? | Letting it go instead of bringing it up again | Reminding each other again and again who was wrong | Telling lots of other people the whole story about it | Keeping a long list of the other person's mistakes
test: me n my bro both want the xbox rn, hes yelling, im so mad. what do i do | Calm down first, then agree to take turns | Yell louder so he gives up | Grab the controller and keep it till he goes away | Unplug it so nobody can play
test: she said sorry but "u started it" lol. does that count | No, a real apology owns up without blaming | Yes, any sorry counts as an apology | Yes, if she said it loudly enough | No, sorry only counts if it is written
test: both kids want to pick the park or the pool, both fine tbh. how do we decide | Flip a coin, both choices are equally good | The oldest kid always decides | Whoever cries first and loudest gets to choose | Go nowhere so nobody is upset
test: theyre pushing each other now over the swing, should i just let em sort it | No, get an adult when someone could get hurt | Yes, pushing is how kids sort things out | Yes, the stronger one will win soon | No, push them both away yourself
test: he's in my face shouting, wanna shove him | Step back and get an adult to help | Shove him first before he does it to you | Shout even louder than him so he backs off | Grab his shirt to stop him from shoving
test: so mad at my friend, gonna send her a mean text | Wait until you are calm, then talk to her | Send it now while you feel it, she deserves it | Send it to the whole group | Send it and delete it later
test: Jake says the one who hits first wins the fight. true? | No, fights hurt both people and solve nothing | Yes, hit first is the rule | Yes, it shows you are the strongest in the year | Yes, unless they are bigger
test: they're saying Liam and Jack had a punch-up over the football and it's all over the group chat | Do not spread it; hitting never solves a quarrel fairly | Spread it, because fights are news | Spread it, so everyone picks a side | Spread it, and say who won and who cried and who threw the first punch
test: my mate says a proper sorry is «sorry, but you started it» | Wrong; a real apology says what you did wrong without adding an excuse | He is right, the «but» makes it fair | He is right, sorry must blame the other | He is right, if you say it loudly so the whole class hears you say it
test: heard Mia and Ava fell out over the swing, they're saying they'll never be friends again lol | Probably not true; friends fall out and can sort it out again | It must be true, falling out ends friendship | It proves swings cause fights | It means they should never play again, not even with other kids
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a compromise? | An arrangement whereby each party concedes a little so that both are fairly satisfied | An arrangement whereby one party obtains everything and the other party obtains nothing at all | A refusal by both parties to continue | A decision made by drawing straws
exam: Identify the defining feature of an «I» sentence. | It expresses one's feelings without blaming the other person | It blames the other person for the problem | It is spoken only to an adult | It begins and ends with an apology, whatever the matter in question
exam: It being given that a disagreement cannot be resolved or a person is being hurt, select the appropriate course of action. | To request the assistance of an adult | To settle the matter by force | To ignore the matter entirely | To post about the matter online
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the solving of problems. | A problem is more readily solved when all parties are calm | A problem is solved fastest by shouting | Calm makes no difference to solving problems | Problems are solved only by the stronger side, whatever the merits of the case
